---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RunFlow from '../components/RunFlow.astro';
import EventLoop from '../components/EventLoop.astro';
import BranchFlow from '../components/BranchFlow.astro';
import AutopilotLog from '../components/AutopilotLog.astro';
import Modes from '../components/Modes.astro';
---
<Base title="How it works: a multi-agent coding workflow, step by step · herdr-orchestra" description="One herdr-orchestra run, step by step: the orchestration plan, the orchestration run, and the pull request.">
  <section class="first">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
      <h1>One run, from request to pull request.</h1>
      <p class="lede">A run builds one feature. The lead writes an orchestration plan of 1 to 4 milestones, the orchestration run builds them on one feature branch, and it ends in a pull request. Here is every step, using a CSV import feature as the example.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="diagram reveal"><RunFlow /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap grid cols-2" style="gap:48px;align-items:start">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Steps 1 to 3 · the orchestration plan</p>
        <h2>You set the goal. The lead writes the plan.</h2>
        <ol class="steps" style="margin-top:28px">
          <li><div><h3>Open your team</h3><p>One pane each: a lead, one or more coders, and an optional reviewer. Coders can run different models. <code>orch agents launch &lt;kind&gt; --model &lt;id&gt;</code> prints each agent's command. Only one tool, or one window? Open just the lead: it builds the team from its own tool (<code>orch team codex</code>) and <code>orch team up</code> opens the other panes when you say go.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>Ask the lead for a feature</h3><p>Run <code>/orch-plan &lt;feature&gt;</code> in the lead's pane. A good request has Feature, Why, Must, Must not, Already decided, Done when.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>The lead researches and plans</h3><p>It reads the codebase, finds the files and patterns involved, and writes milestones as issues. Anything it can't decide goes under Open questions, with options.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>The lead picks a tier for each milestone</h3><p>Small for mechanical work, medium for normal implementation, high where reasoning is the hard part. It reads the model catalog (<code>orch models</code>) and writes the reason next to each choice.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>You read the plan and say go</h3><p><code>.orchestrate/plan.md</code> is a normal Markdown file. Change dependencies, a tier, review yes or no, and answer open questions. Nothing starts before you say so.</p></div></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="term">
        <div class="bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>.orchestrate/plan.md</span></div>
        <pre><span class="t-lead"># Plan: CSV import for transactions</span>
Branch: feature/csv-import
Check command: npm test
Isolation: worktree
Estimate: 1h10m
Lead: lead (claude: opus)
Workers: claude-1 (claude: sonnet), claude-2 (claude: haiku)
Reviewer: reviewer (agy)

<span class="t-lead">## [x] M2: Import endpoint</span>
<span class="t-dim">Depends on:</span> M1 (shared ImportRow type)
<span class="t-dim">Tier:</span> medium
<span class="t-dim">Needs:</span> coding, tool-use
<span class="t-dim">Model why:</span> one module, a clear contract,
  an endpoint to copy; no design decisions
<span class="t-dim">Estimate:</span> 40m
<span class="t-dim">Review:</span> yes
<span class="t-dim">Goal:</span> users upload a bank CSV and get rows back
<span class="t-dim">Acceptance criteria:</span>
- POST /imports returns 400 when the
  amount column is missing
<span class="t-dim">Interfaces:</span>
- ImportRow {'{'} date, amount, payee {'}'}
<span class="t-dim">Open questions:</span>
- none
<span class="t-dim">Notes from user:</span>
<span class="t-coder">  Use the existing upload limit (10 MB).</span></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap grid cols-2" style="gap:48px;align-items:start">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Step 4 · the orchestration run</p>
        <h2>The lead dispatches. The team builds, checks and reviews.</h2>
        <ol class="steps" style="margin-top:28px">
          <li><div><h3>Dispatch, then pause</h3><p>The lead runs <code>orch dispatch</code> and stops. Each milestone goes to a free coder whose model serves its tier. In worktree mode, independent milestones run at once.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>Check</h3><p>When a coder finishes, your check command runs on its work. A failure sends the last 60 lines back, up to 3 rounds. Out of rounds, the milestone gets one go on a stronger model.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>Review</h3><p>The reviewer reads the diff and reports blockers. Each blocker round becomes a fix job for the next free coder, up to 2 rounds.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>Land</h3><p>An approved milestone becomes one commit on the feature branch, and the milestones that depended on it unlock.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>Wake the lead</h3><p>The lead is woken with what happened: a milestone landed, a coder asked a question, something failed or is held. It decides, dispatches what is ready, and pauses again.</p></div></li>
        </ol>
        <p class="diagram-note">A plain script, the event manager (<code>orch autopilot</code>), runs this loop; the lead makes its decisions. <a href="/docs/driving/">The orchestration run →</a></p>
      </div>
      <AutopilotLog />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Inside step 4 · the event manager</p>
      <h2>The lead is woken like a browser's event loop.</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:36px">In a browser, a click doesn't run straight away: its callback waits in a queue, and the event loop runs it only when the call stack is empty. A run works the same way. Agents finish work or ask questions in the background, each event is stored in a queue, and the event manager wakes the lead once with the whole batch when it is idle. The lead decides, and an answer goes back to the coder that asked.</p>
      <div class="diagram"><EventLoop /></div>
      <p class="diagram-note">The event kinds and commands are the real ones. <a href="/docs/lead/">The lead and the event manager →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Step 5</p>
      <h2>One branch, one pull request.</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:36px">The run never adds bookkeeping branches or commits to your repo. You get a <code>feature/&lt;name&gt;</code> branch with a real commit per milestone and a summary in <code>.orchestrate/summary.md</code>, including the tier planned for each milestone next to the model that really did it, and the estimated time next to the real time, with the reasons when something took longer. Run <code>orch finish</code> and it re-checks the branch, pushes it and opens the pull request.</p>
      <div class="diagram reveal"><BranchFlow /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">When it needs you</p>
      <h2>You answer, the same coder carries on.</h2>
      <p class="lede">If a coder asks something the plan and the code don't answer, the lead is woken and asks you in its pane. Your answer goes into the plan with <code>orch answer</code> and back to the coder that asked, which continues from its saved work. Other milestones keep running, and no event is lost while anyone is away. <a href="/docs/when-it-stops/">When a run needs you →</a></p>
      <p class="lede">After the summary, ask the lead for any change you want. It runs <code>orch fix M1 "the login button should be blue"</code>: one small follow-up job goes straight to the coder who built M1, the check and review run as usual, and the summary is written again. <a href="/docs/lead/">The lead and the event manager →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Modes</p>
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:32px">Pick per run.</h2>
      <Modes />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
